 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.06666666666666667,
   "dur": 3.439,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "Which has more pigment cells? Count them, row by row."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 4.5,
   "dur": 12.157,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "Most would guess the darkest. But in the bottom row of skin, each pigment cell feeds melanin to a few dozen neighbours. Across all three, the number comes out about the same."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 17.566666666666666,
   "dur": 8.688,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "What changes is the work each one does. In deeper skin, they make more of the dark melanin, in larger grains, handed out one by one."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 27.366666666666667,
   "dur": 8.223,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "So why would the work differ? Nina Jablonski and George Chaplin set skin colour beside sunlight, all around the world, and the two matched."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 38.1,
   "dur": 8.988,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "Their idea: under fierce sun, more melanin protected a vitamin called folate. Under weak sun, less of it let enough light through to make vitamin D."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo06",
   "t": 50.7,
   "dur": 6.961,
   "file": "vo/06.wav",
   "text": "And on any one part of the body, the pigment cells sit about a third of a paper's thickness apart."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo07",
   "t": 58.63333333333333,
   "dur": 5.738,
   "file": "vo/07.wav",
   "text": "So, which has more? None of them. Same cells, every colour, each answering its own sky."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo08",
   "t": 65.63333333333334,
   "dur": 5.141,
   "file": "vo/08.wav",
   "text": "Next: skin answers the cold, too. Why goosebumps, with almost no fur?"
  }
 ]
